##
    Numbers written down that no number type can hold.

    This program does not compile. Every one of these is a well-formed number as far as reading
    it goes — the digits are digits — and it is only turning them into a value that finds there
    is no value to turn them into. So the scanner is quiet and the refusal comes later, which is
    the opposite way round from most of the mistakes in this folder.

    The second one is the one worth the visit. '-9223372036854775808' is the most negative
    integer written out, and it does not work: the minus is a separate operator, so what the
    compiler reads is the number after it, which is one past the largest. That is exactly why
    Integer.MinValue is a name and not something a reader can spell in digits.

    A float is deliberately absent from this file. It is the one number type with a value for a
    number too large — Float.Infinity — and its own arithmetic already produces that value, so a
    float literal saturates rather than being refused.
##

shared model Program

    function Main()
        Program.PastTheLargest();
        Program.TheMostNegative();
        Program.TooManyDigits();
        Program.PastAReal();
        Program.HalvesTooLarge();
        Program.OverZero();
    end function

    # One past Integer.MaxValue. A decimal point would read the same digits as a real.
    function PastTheLargest()
        integer counted = 9223372036854775808;
        Console.WriteLine(counted);
    end function

    # The most negative integer, and the reason it has a name.
    function TheMostNegative()
        integer floor = -9223372036854775808;
        Console.WriteLine(floor);
    end function

    # Seventeen digits in base sixteen, where an integer has room for sixteen.
    function TooManyDigits()
        integer mask = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF;
        Console.WriteLine(mask);
    end function

    # Past Real.MaxValue, which a float would reach with fewer digits kept.
    function PastAReal()
        real measured = 999999999999999999999999999999.0;
        Console.WriteLine(measured);
    end function

    # Both halves of a fraction are whole numbers, and this one has a half that is not.
    function HalvesTooLarge()
        fraction ratio = 99999999999999999999|7;
        Console.WriteLine(ratio);
    end function

    # The bar in a fraction is division, so nothing goes under it but a number that is not zero.
    function OverZero()
        fraction undefined = 1|0;
        Console.WriteLine(undefined);
    end function

end model
